.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt XFT_THREADS 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_thread_spawn ,
.Nm xft_thread_join ,
.Nm xft_thread_detach ,
.Nm xft_thread_id ,
.Nm xft_thread_destroy
.Nd threads
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In threads.h
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_thread_spawn "t_thread *thread" "t_sysinfo sysinfo" "t_thread_opts opts"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_thread_join "t_thread *thread"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_thread_detach "t_thread *thread"
.Ft t_thread_id
.Fn xft_thread_id "const t_thread *thread"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_thread_destroy "t_thread_instance *instance"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions start threads that share the address space, open files and
signal handlers of the process.
Linux issues
.Xr clone 2
directly, a build with
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
calls
.Xr pthread_create 3 ,
and Windows calls
.Ql CreateThread .
.Ss Types
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef void (*t_thread_func)(t_any);

typedef struct s_thread_opts {
	t_thread_func       fn;
	t_size              stack_size;
	t_any               argument;
} t_thread_opts;
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
and on Windows,
.Vt t_thread
holds the thread's handle by value and nothing else is allocated:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef t_uptr t_thread_id;

typedef struct s_thread {
	t_thread_id         handle;
} t_thread;
.Ed
.Pp
On Linux without the C library,
.Vt t_thread
points to the thread's state, which lives in the memory of the thread itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef t_u32a t_thread_id;

typedef enum e_thread_status : t_u8 {
	THREAD_RUNNING,
	THREAD_DETACHED,
	THREAD_COMPLETED,
} t_thread_status;

typedef struct s_thread_instance {
	t_size              size;
	t_thread_func       fn;
	t_any               argument;
	t_thread_id         tid;
	t_thread_status     status;
	t_any               base;
} t_thread_instance;

typedef struct s_thread {
	t_thread_instance   *instance;
} t_thread;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_thread_opts
names the function the thread runs, its argument and the size of its stack.
.Dv XFT_THREAD_STACKSIZE
is 8388608, 8 MiB, unless it is defined before
.In threads.h
is included.
.Pp
.Vt t_thread_instance ,
.Vt t_thread_status
and
.Fn xft_thread_destroy
exist only on Linux without the C library, so code that touches them does
not compile for the other targets.
.Fa base
and
.Fa size
describe the region that holds the thread,
.Fa tid
is its kernel thread ID, and
.Fa status
starts as
.Dv THREAD_RUNNING
and is read and written atomically.
.Ss Functions
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
starts a thread that calls
.Fa opts.fn
with
.Fa opts.argument
and stores its state in
.Fa thread .
Linux reads the page size and the thread-local storage layout from
.Fa sysinfo ,
which comes from
.Fn xft_get_sysinfo
of the running process; the other targets ignore it.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_join
waits for the thread to return from its function and frees its memory.
Everything the thread wrote is visible to the caller once
.Fn xft_thread_join
returns.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_detach
gives the thread's memory to the thread itself, which frees it when its
function returns.
When the function has already returned,
.Fn xft_thread_detach
frees the memory at once.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_id
returns the thread's identifier: the kernel thread ID on Linux without the C
library, which reads 0 once the thread has exited, the
.Vt pthread_t
with
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
and the
.Ql HANDLE
on Windows.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_destroy
frees
.Fa instance
and the memory around it and ends the calling thread.
The thread entry calls it when the function of a detached thread returns;
a detached thread may also call it on its own
.Vt t_thread_instance
to end early.
.Pp
Every spawned thread is joined or detached exactly once, and
.Fa thread
is invalid after either.
.Ss Completion
On Linux without the C library, when its function returns, the thread swaps
.Fa status
to
.Dv THREAD_COMPLETED ,
and
.Fn xft_thread_detach
swaps it to
.Dv THREAD_DETACHED ,
both with an acquire-release exchange.
Whichever side sees the other's value frees the memory: a thread that finds
.Dv THREAD_DETACHED
calls
.Fn xft_thread_destroy ,
and a detach that finds
.Dv THREAD_COMPLETED
frees it from the caller.
.Ss Linux
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
maps one private, anonymous region of
.Fa size
bytes with
.Xr mmap 2
and
.Dv PROT_NONE ,
then makes all of it but the first page readable and writable with
.Xr mprotect 2 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
base                                                     base + size
| guard | stack          | TLS block | t_thread_instance | padding |
                         ^ initial stack pointer
.Ed
.Pp
The guard page faults on a stack overflow.
The stack takes
.Fa opts.stack_size
bytes, at least one page, rounded up to a multiple of the page size.
The TLS block is aligned to
.Fa sysinfo._linux.tls_align ,
which is the alignment of the
.Dv PT_TLS
segment and at least the constructive interference size, so the blocks of
two threads never share a cache line.
The whole region is a multiple of the page size.
.Pp
The TLS block takes the static TLS layout of the target: on x86_64 the
thread-local data sits below a 16-byte thread control block whose first word
points to itself, and on aarch64 the 16-byte control block sits below the
data.
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
copies the
.Fa sysinfo._linux.tls_filesz
bytes of the initialized image into the block; the rest of the block is
zero, so each thread sees
.Vt _Thread_local
variables at their initial values.
The main thread gets its block from
.Fn xft_start_tls_for_main_thread ;
see
.Xr xft_rt 3 .
.Pp
The thread is created with
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
.Dv CLONE_FS ,
.Dv CLONE_FILES ,
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND ,
.Dv CLONE_THREAD ,
.Dv CLONE_SYSVSEM ,
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS ,
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID
and
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID ,
through
.Fn xft_clone ;
see
.Xr xft_os 3 .
Both thread ID pointers point to
.Fa tid ,
so the kernel stores the thread ID before
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
returns, and clears it and wakes a futex on it when the thread exits.
.Fn xft_thread_join
waits on that futex with
.Fn xft_futex_wait
until
.Fa tid
reads 0, then unmaps the region with
.Xr munmap 2 .
The thread inherits the signal mask of the thread that spawned it.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_destroy
runs on the stack it frees, so it is written in inline assembly for x86_64
and aarch64.
It clears the thread's clear-child-tid address with
.Xr set_tid_address 2 ,
so the kernel does not write to the freed region, blocks every signal with
.Xr rt_sigprocmask 2 ,
so no handler runs on it, then issues
.Xr munmap 2
and
.Xr exit 2
without touching the stack in between.
.Ss XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
calls
.Xr pthread_create 3
with a stack of
.Fa opts.stack_size
bytes set through
.Xr pthread_attr_setstacksize 3 ;
the C library provides the stack, its guard and thread-local storage.
The function and its argument travel to the thread in a structure on the
stack of
.Fn xft_thread_spawn ,
which holds a locked
.Vt t_mutex ;
the thread copies both and unlocks it, and
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
locks it again with
.Dv xft_lock_fast
before it returns, so nothing outlives the call.
The wait spins briefly, then sleeps until the thread unlocks it.
.Fn xft_thread_join
calls
.Xr pthread_join 3 ,
and
.Fn xft_thread_detach
calls
.Xr pthread_detach 3 ,
which leaves freeing the thread to the C library.
.Pp
glibc older than 2.34 keeps the thread functions in
.Pa libpthread ;
executables built by Zig link it automatically, and linking the archive by
hand there needs
.Fl lpthread .
glibc 2.34 and later and musl keep them in the C library itself.
.Ss Windows
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
calls
.Ql CreateThread
with
.Dv STACK_SIZE_PARAM_IS_A_RESERVATION ,
so
.Fa opts.stack_size
is the reserved size of the stack, and 0 takes the default of the
executable.
The function and its argument reach the thread through the same
.Vt t_mutex
handoff as with
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC .
.Fn xft_thread_join
waits with
.Ql WaitForSingleObject
and
.Dv INFINITE ,
then closes the handle with
.Ql CloseHandle .
.Fn xft_thread_detach
closes the handle, and the system frees the thread when it ends.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
returns
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
when the memory cannot be mapped or the thread cannot be created; on failure
it frees what it allocated and leaves
.Fa thread
unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn xft_thread_id
returns the thread's identifier.
.Fn xft_thread_join
and
.Fn xft_thread_detach
return nothing, and
.Fn xft_thread_destroy
does not return.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compute a value on a second thread and wait for it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static void	work(t_any arg)
{
	*(t_u64 *)arg = 42;
}

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	t_thread	thread;
	t_u64		value;

	value = 0;
	if (!xft_thread_spawn(&thread, xft_get_sysinfo(sp),
			(t_thread_opts){.fn = work,
			.stack_size = XFT_THREAD_STACKSIZE, .argument = &value}))
		xft_exit_process(1);
	xft_thread_join(&thread);
	xft_exit_process(value != 42);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_atomics 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft_rt 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet
.It
On Linux, a thread gets storage only for the
.Vt _Thread_local
variables of the executable, not for those of shared libraries.
.It
Call
.Fn xft_thread_destroy
only from a detached thread, on its own
.Vt t_thread_instance .
On any other thread it frees memory that a later
.Fn xft_thread_join
reads.
.It
With
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC ,
a stack size below
.Dv PTHREAD_STACK_MIN
is silently ignored, and the thread gets the default stack size of the C
library.
.El
